1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
TAMARA: Shall we go through the notes we've made from our research into loneliness now, Dev?

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,750
DEV: OK, Tamara. It's been a real eye-opener.

3
00:00:08,750 --> 00:00:15,000
I had no idea that loneliness has been increasing steadily for the last twenty years.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:19,140
TAMARA: I know. And it's the same all over the world

5
00:00:19,540 --> 00:00:22,650
the downside of a modern lifestyle I guess.

6
00:00:22,650 --> 00:00:27,810
DEV: Did you come to any conclusions about what the reasons for the increase are?

7
00:00:27,810 --> 00:00:32,930
TAMARA: Well, I'd assumed it was mainly an issue for the elderly -

8
00:00:32,930 --> 00:00:38,050
but in fact it's something which affects young people just as much.

9
00:00:38,050 --> 00:00:42,314
DEV: Mmm. So nothing really to do with longer lifespans.

10
00:00:42,314 --> 00:00:46,040
What about social media? In my case

11
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:49,990
far from making me feel isolated, it actually does the opposite.

12
00:00:49,990 --> 00:00:52,790
TAMARA: It definitely does more good than harm.

13
00:00:52,790 --> 00:00:58,040
I'd say loneliness has a lot to do with the way cities are designed -

14
00:00:58,040 --> 00:01:02,940
people living in high-rise flats with not much opportunity to speak to their neighbours.

15
00:01:02,940 --> 00:01:04,330
DEV: I think you're right.

16
00:01:04,330 --> 00:01:08,988
TAMARA: Another possible reason is that people are having fewer children

17
00:01:08,988 --> 00:01:12,800
and don't live in large extended family groups.

18
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:14,880
DEV: But, in this country anyway

19
00:01:14,960 --> 00:01:20,530
that all changed decades ago and yet loneliness is a more recent problem.

20
00:01:20,530 --> 00:01:27,718
TAMARA: I suppose so. A more plausible explanation is that people are having to move around for work

21
00:01:27,718 --> 00:01:32,510
and often end up living miles away from their family and friends.

22
00:01:32,510 --> 00:01:33,890
DEV: That's true.

23
00:01:33,890 --> 00:01:37,890
TAMARA: Looking at the studies on health risks and loneliness,

24
00:01:37,890 --> 00:01:43,890
there are claims that loneliness has as much impact as smoking 15 cigarettes a day.

25
00:01:43,890 --> 00:01:47,052
DEV: Or similar to the risks caused by obesity.

26
00:01:47,052 --> 00:01:51,970
But I'm not sure there's enough evidence for some of these claims.

27
00:01:51,970 --> 00:01:55,312
TAMARA: Well - what about that one in Finland

28
00:01:55,312 --> 00:02:01,162
which showed that loneliness increased the risk of cancer by about ten per cent?

29
00:02:01,162 --> 00:02:05,340
And those findings have been supported by other studies too.

30
00:02:05,340 --> 00:02:07,340
DEV: You're right about that one

31
00:02:07,360 --> 00:02:12,374
I was actually thinking of the studies on dementia.

32
00:02:12,374 --> 00:02:15,460
Some found no association between loneliness and dementia

33
00:02:15,620 --> 00:02:18,090
and others found the opposite.

34
00:02:18,090 --> 00:02:20,516
TAMARA: Not exactly reliable then.

35
00:02:20,516 --> 00:02:25,000
There's been a lot of research on cardiovascular disease

36
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:28,280
and whether loneliness contributes to that.

37
00:02:28,280 --> 00:02:37,165
DEV: Yes, I read that it was hard to reach a judgement as the definition of loneliness varied quite a lot

38
00:02:37,165 --> 00:02:40,550
and the responses from participants were too subjective.

39
00:02:40,550 --> 00:02:46,050
But there's no doubt that loneliness contributes to a weakened immune system.

40
00:02:46,050 --> 00:02:49,970
TAMARA: Unquestionably. The data on that is sound.

41
00:02:49,970 --> 00:02:54,330
DEV: What did you think about the evolutionary theory of loneliness?

42
00:02:54,330 --> 00:02:57,340
TAMARA: Well, I thought the idea that loneliness evolved

43
00:02:57,340 --> 00:03:01,017
because it motivated people to be

44
00:03:01,017 --> 00:03:05,920
with other people quite convincing. Survival often depended on group cooperation.

45
00:03:05,920 --> 00:03:13,452
DEV: But I don't think there's enough evidence to claim that there must be a group of neurons in our brains

46
00:03:13,452 --> 00:03:17,710
which influence social behaviour, by making us feel bad when we're alone.

47
00:03:17,710 --> 00:03:20,900
TAMARA: There are a few studies which support the theory

48
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:25,480
but not conclusively enough. More evidence is needed.

49
00:03:25,480 --> 00:03:29,300
DEV: Mmm. And anyway, this theory's not really useful

50
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:33,010
when it comes to solving the problem of loneliness today.

51
00:03:33,010 --> 00:03:34,570
TAMARA: True.

52
00:03:34,570 --> 00:03:39,610
DEV: Should we look at the relationship between loneliness and mental health now?

53
00:03:39,610 --> 00:03:44,458
TAMARA: OK. So, loneliness and depression are clearly related

54
00:03:44,458 --> 00:03:50,382
and that's been recognised by various governments around the world.

55
00:03:50,382 --> 00:03:55,230
But unlike depression, loneliness has no recognised clinical form.

56
00:03:55,230 --> 00:03:58,940
DEV: There's no available diagnosis or effective treatment

57
00:03:58,940 --> 00:04:01,350
and that's not likely to change.

58
00:04:01,350 --> 00:04:03,852
TAMARA: I don't think so either.

59
00:04:03,852 --> 00:04:09,214
I was thinking we should start our presentation with an example from our own experience.

60
00:04:09,214 --> 00:04:14,218
I'd like to talk about how lonely I was when I started university.

61
00:04:14,218 --> 00:04:18,150
Being away from home for the first time and all that.

62
00:04:18,150 --> 00:04:22,435
DEV: Good idea. Everyone will be able to relate to that -

63
00:04:22,435 --> 00:04:27,500
although a lot of students were probably too embarrassed to admit to it.

64
00:04:27,500 --> 00:04:32,480
TAMARA: Yeah. We could discuss ways of dealing with loneliness as well

65
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:35,720
Like just talking to strangers.

66
00:04:35,720 --> 00:04:39,404
DEV: Loads of studies have shown that interactions

67
00:04:39,404 --> 00:04:45,390
with shop assistants and bar staff make people feel more optimistic and relaxed.

68
00:04:45,390 --> 00:04:47,200
TAMARA: I don't know about that

69
00:04:47,200 --> 00:04:51,800
but it must make people feel more connected with their community.

70
00:04:51,800 --> 00:04:58,900
DEV: True. Although you need to be a certain kind of person to be able to just strike up a conversation.

71
00:04:58,900 --> 00:05:02,581
TAMARA: Good point. We should say something about solitude

72
00:05:02,581 --> 00:05:07,488
and how being alone and being lonely aren't the same thing.

73
00:05:07,488 --> 00:05:14,440
It's strange the way some people can't stand being by themselves while others love it.

74
00:05:14,440 --> 00:05:21,497
DEV: Yeah. The research shows a certain amount of solitude is beneficial for well-being, which I appreciate -

75
00:05:21,497 --> 00:05:26,100
but being alone isn't something I actually like

76
00:05:26,600 --> 00:05:29,800
I'd never choose to go on holiday alone, for example.

77
00:05:29,800 --> 00:05:31,260
TAMARA: Me neither.

78
00:05:31,260 --> 00:05:33,820
DEV: Well let's not...
